%I A019347 #21 Nov 20 2026 12:29:45
%S A019347 3,13,17,23,37,43,47,53,67,73,83,103,107,113,137,157,163,167,173,223,
%T A019347 227,233,257,263,277,283,293,313,317,337,347,353,367,383,397,433,443,
%U A019347 463,467,487,503,547,557,563,587,593,607,613,647,653,673,677,683,727,743,773,797
%N A019347 Primes with primitive root 20.
%C A019347 On Artin's conjecture, the number of terms <= N is asymptotically equivalent to (20/19)*C*primepi(N), where C = A005596 is Artin's constant. For example, below 1000000 there are 78496 primes not equal to 2 or 5, among which 30890 are terms of this sequence, and we see the proportion is close to (20/19)*C. In addition, my comments in A019335 also apply here. - _Jianing Song_, Nov 20 2026
%H A019347 Vincenzo Librandi, <a href="/A019347/b019347.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A019347 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ArtinsConstant.html">Artin's constant</a>.
%H A019347 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artin%27s_conjecture_on_primitive_roots">Artin's conjecture on primitive roots</a>.
%H A019347 <a href="/index/Pri#primes_root">Index entries for primes by primitive root</a>.
%t A019347 pr=20; Select[Prime[Range[200]], MultiplicativeOrder[pr, # ] == #-1 &]
%t A019347 (* Alternative: *)
%t A019347 Join[{3,13,17},Select[Prime[Range[200]],PrimitiveRoot[#,20]==20&]] (* _Harvey P. Dale_, Mar 22 2019 *)
%Y A019347 (PARI) isprimroot(p, b=20) = isprime(p) && b%p!=0 && znorder(Mod(b, p)) == p-1 \\ _Jianing Song_, Nov 20 2026
%K A019347 nonn,changed
%O A019347 1,1
%A A019347 _David W. Wilson_
